From f82b6cae9b9813132c3a362e99191677d50310e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Montana <montana@linux.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 08:59:14 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] bngsim: hold a rate that reads an observable until a firing
 is done

A firing takes each product molecule out of its Molecules observables,
transforms it, puts it back, and then puts the products back in their
reactions, one molecule at a time. Until that is done the system is in no
state it ever occupies, and the rates that read an observable were
computed there: with one X, kgap*(1+9*Xp/Xtot) was 0/0, the NaN went into
a_tot, where a_tot - old + new keeps it, and the run ended silently at
the first X firing.

This is RuleWorld/nfsim#117 (issue #115, merged upstream as a0bf9134),
written against the pinned base: Observable's four add and subtract
loops, MoleculeType::removeFromRxns, updateRxnMembership and
updateConnectedRxnMembership ask System::holdFiringPropensityUpdate()
first, and ReactionClass::fire computes what was held once the products
are back in their reactions. The base has no selector->updateBatch, so
the held reactions are given to selector->update one at a time. Beyond
upstream's change: if a firing is left by an exception (bngsim turns
NFsim's fatal exits into exceptions, and a session outlives one) the
hold is dropped, so it does not outlive the firing. Drop this carry when
the base moves past a0bf9134, keeping that part if upstream lacks it.
